MODE      cm-threads-spawn
FORMS     0x26 thread.index, 0x27 thread.new-indirect, 0x28 thread.resume-later
          — and nothing else, which is why this fixture exists: ADR 0100's point 4 lands one form
          at a time, so each slice needs a fixture using only the forms it models. switch.wat needs
          all eight; index-call.wat needs one.
RESULT    529  (0x211)
EXIT      0
RUNS      30, identical, on the CORRECTED fixture (callback re-YIELDs until the body's marker is
          set). The first version, which packed on the first re-entry, also gave 529 in 30 of 30 —
          see "The execution order" below for why that agreement was luck about wasmtime, not a
          property of the reference.

PACKING   bit 0 the spawned body's marker; bits 4-7 the entry Thread's index; bits 8-11 the body's.

DECODED
  marker            1   the spawned Thread ran
  entry index       1   the entry (implicit) Thread
  body index        2   the Thread created by thread.new-indirect

WHAT IT PINS, and the second line is what index-call.reading could not reach
  * The entry Thread is 1 and the spawned Thread is 2 — the reference's Table policy showing
    through: array starts as [None], so index 0 is reserved and allocations run 1, 2.
  * CONCURRENT DISTINCT INDICES. The entry Thread yields rather than returning, so it is still
    registered while the spawned Thread runs: two Threads live at once, holding 1 and 2. An
    implementation that freed the entry's slot at the yield would hand the body a 1 and pass a
    reachability check while being wrong about observable guest state.

WHAT IT DOES NOT PIN
  * The slot-reuse ORDER. Nothing is freed before the call ends, so no reuse policy is
    discriminated — the same gap switch.reading and index-call.reading record for their own
    reasons. A fixture holding two Threads live and freeing them out of order would.
  * The execution order — and the first version of this reading CLAIMED that and was wrong.

    It said the value was order-independent because "each index is written by its own Thread".
    That was true of the writes and false of the read: the callback packed the indices on its FIRST
    re-entry, assuming the spawned body had run by then. The reference does not promise that. Its
    YIELD arm, in canon_lift's callback case:

        inst.exclusive_thread = None
        match code:
          case CallbackCode.YIELD:
            thread.wait_until(lambda: inst.exclusive_thread is None)

    The predicate is already true, and Thread.wait_until begins

        if ready_func() and not DETERMINISTIC_PROFILE and random.randint(0,1):
          return

    so half the time it returns at once and the callback is re-entered with EVENT_NONE before the
    body has run. A conforming implementation taking that branch would have produced 0x011 — body
    field never written — from the first version.

    wasmtime 49.0.2 never takes it here: 30 of 30 runs of the first version gave 529. So the first
    reading pinned ONE IMPLEMENTATION'S FIXED SCHEDULING CHOICE, not a property of the reference.

    CORRECTED: the callback now re-YIELDs while mem[0] (the body's marker) is 0, and packs only once
    it is set. Every order now reaches the same packed value, so the result IS order-independent —
    by the loop, not by assumption. Re-taken: 30 of 30 runs give 529, exit 0.

    Still worth stating: wasmtime exercises only the branch where the body runs first, so the
    re-YIELD path is correct by construction and NOT yet exercised by any implementation. Burroughs
    will be the first to exercise it if its scheduling point at YIELD re-enters before running the
    ready Thread — and the current engine, which re-enters at once with no scheduling point at all,
    would loop forever against this fixture. That is the defect made visible, not a fixture bug.

GATE OFF (wasmtime)
  `thread.index` requires the component model threading feature (at offset 0x47)
  exit 1 — a parse refusal naming the first 🧵 production in the canon section

BURROUGHS, before this slice
  burroughs: feature not implemented in this phase: component: canon 1: canon thread.resume-later
  (0x28) is a shared-everything-threads built-in, which this engine does not support — the
  component is well-formed and no gate admits this proposal
  exit 5 — refused by name at decode, because 0x27 and 0x28 are not modeled yet

PROVENANCE
  taken      2026-10-11T20:34:55Z (re-taken on the corrected fixture; first version 20:06:34Z)
  host       Darwin arm64
  re-taken   wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), by build.sh on 2026-10-17, one run: unchanged
  wasmtime   wasmtime 49.0.2 (3c8a3e79a 2026-10-02)
  flags      -W component-model-threading=y -W component-model-async=y
  invoke     --invoke 'run(0)'
  wasm-tools wasm-tools 1.258.0
  source     spawn.wat -> spawn.wasm, hand-authored WAT, no Rust toolchain
